  "narrative": {
    "intro": "AmerisourceBergen Drug has federal contract obligations of $4.8B in FY2026 to date. The company won 305,111 awards so far in the fiscal year, with an average award size of $15.7K. Obligations fell 35.5% year over year. Obligations by fiscal year show a buildup to FY25 followed by a pullback in FY26: FY21 $2.1B, FY22 $2B, FY23 $4.4B, FY24 $6.1B, FY25 $7.4B, FY26 $4.8B. The Department of Defense is the dominant buyer, with obligations of $4.8B. The Department of Veterans Affairs bought $639K and the Department of Justice bought $28K. Market-level obligations concentrate in manufacturing and distribution: Medicinal and Botanical Manufacturing accounts for $3.3B, Drugs and Druggists' Sundries Merchant Wholesalers account for $1.6B, and Pharmaceutical Preparation Manufacturing accounts for $639K. All figures are contract obligations except award-value figures in the largest-awards and expiring-contracts tables, which are total contract values. Current-year totals are partial and reflect fiscal year to date activity.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "How has AmerisourceBergen Drug's federal business trended this year?",
        "body": "AmerisourceBergen Drug obligated $4.8B in FY2026 to date, down 35.5% year over year. Obligations rose from FY21 $2.1B to a peak of FY25 $7.4B before falling to FY26 $4.8B. Award volume is high, with 305,111 awards and an average award size of $15.7K. The drop this fiscal year follows the elevated obligations recorded in FY24 and FY25."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Who are the main buyers and what markets drive obligations?",
        "body": "The Department of Defense obligated $4.8B from AmerisourceBergen Drug in FY2026 to date. The Department of Veterans Affairs obligated $639K and the Department of Justice obligated $28K. Medicinal and Botanical Manufacturing drives most obligations at $3.3B, followed by Drugs and Druggists' Sundries Merchant Wholesalers at $1.6B, and Pharmaceutical Preparation Manufacturing at $639K."
      }
    ]
  },
